                        "form": "more inosculate"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "ad9eafe8-e1f9-4c26-8444-a849364afac5",
                        "entry_id": "dd02dca6-958e-3301-9465-ae19b0b55547",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most inosculate"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b7a801e6-d4f6-305e-858c-30a94a67c8a8",
                        "entry_id": "dd02dca6-958e-3301-9465-ae19b0b55547",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pertaining to or characterized by inosculation."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "59966c05-c502-476c-95d9-a765b5bb6f5f",
                                "sense_id": "b7a801e6-d4f6-305e-858c-30a94a67c8a8",
                                "text": "However, not all physicians of that age were convinced about the inosculate nature of these diseases, and debate continued unabated until the tragic self-experimentation of John Hunter in 1767."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d7db26b-552f-4b87-bfe0-23c4f2582b4b",
                                "sense_id": "b7a801e6-d4f6-305e-858c-30a94a67c8a8",
                                "text": "The choice between normal, inverse, and inosculate strategies required some attention if only to emphasize the difference between the inosculate approach and much early work on numerical taxonomy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b5c8bd2f-6ac6-4c59-9eb2-07964c1786c6",
                                "sense_id": "b7a801e6-d4f6-305e-858c-30a94a67c8a8",
                                "text": "This study proposes to trace the inosculate nature of the mask theme in Nietzsche's philosophy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04768b20-3a0d-4539-b0cf-dc8d568c0c53",
                                "sense_id": "b7a801e6-d4f6-305e-858c-30a94a67c8a8",
                                "text": "The trunks of inosculate, or self-grafting, trees, such as Elm, Live Oak, and Dogwood, are the load-bearing structure, and the branches from a continuous lattice frame for the walls and roof."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
